#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Some Assembly Required: a tile-based romantic comedy about a lonely
human, a very expensive robot, and the week everything changes, in
Python + Tk.

Standard library only.  The engine is the one Miskatonic, Hyperspace
Homeroom, Clover Vale, Lazarus Deep, Larkspur Hall, Nine Lives and
Penultimate share (one hero, a map of squares, people to talk to by
keyword, shops, places underneath), and the rules are The Black Hack's:
six stats rolled on 3d6, and everything you try a d20 rolled under the
stat that fits; troublemakers never roll to hit, you roll to get out of
the way; Armor Points that soak up damage until you rest; usage dice for
the things that run out; and a level for every adventure you live
through.

Bellwether, a sunny suburb, 2041.  Robots do most things now: they take
your order at the drive-thru, sweep the streets and deal the cards.  But a
Daffodil isn't a robot like that.  Daffodils are made to be someone: a
companion, a friend, a lover, whatever you design.  You've had a long,
lonely couple of years.  You've been looking at the Daffodil website at two
in the morning.  You've got savings.  On Saturday you go to the Daffodil
store at the Galleria and design her: face, figure, hair, voice, temper,
the works.  She comes in a box.  You press the button on the back of her
neck and count to three.

Then there's the rest of the week: clothes for her (she arrives with
none), the gym (she's decided you need it), dinner with your sister (who
has Opinions about robots), a night at the casino (she can count cards),
Daffodils going missing all over town, and on Sunday at midnight, Update
7.2, which somebody has rigged to wipe every companion Daffodil back to
the factory.  Including yours.

The design, her name, and who she becomes are yours.  Every new game
makes a new week: who is who about town, what she likes, where the clues
are, and the Random Events.  The game is saved to ~/.daffodil.json
whenever you change place and when you quit, and resumed next time.

Rolls (The Black Hack)
  Stats: STR, DEX, CON, INT, WIS and CHA, rolled on 3d6 in order (after a
  15 or better the next is rolled on 2d6+2), and two swapped.  To do
  something hard, roll a d20: under the stat succeeds.  Advantage rolls
  two and keeps the lower, Disadvantage the higher.  A powerful opponent
  adds 1 to your roll for every Hit Die above your level.

Fights
  Attack: roll under STR, close up (DEX to zap or throw).  When something
  attacks you, roll under STR (or DEX, from range) to get out of the way;
  fail and it hits you for damage by its Hit Dice.  Armor Points soak it
  up until you rest an hour.  An attack roll of 1 doubles the damage; a
  20 when you're dodging doubles theirs.  At 0 HP you're Out of Action:
  roll on the table and hope for "just knocked out".

Your Daffodil
  She has her own stats (from the design), Hit Dice equal to your level,
  her own HP, and a charge on a usage die.  Talk to her: she learns, and
  her Learning goes from the dictionary to something very like a person.
  Affection, 0 to 10, is how she feels about you.

Touch and mouse
  Tap a square to walk there, tap next to you to step, and tap the menus.
  Gamepad (Linux, the Steam Deck): the d-pad or stick moves, A waits or
  chooses, B backs out, X uses an app or a trick, Y Luck, LB uses
  something, RB the journal, View the map and Start the menu.

Sound effects and music are synthesized and played with afplay (macOS),
paplay or aplay if one is available, on Android with pyjnius, or with
pygame's mixer; otherwise the game is silent.  Music is cached in
~/.cache/daffodil.

App bundle (macOS):  daffodil --build-app [DIR]   builds daffodil.app
                     daffodil --icon-png SIZE [OUT.png]

Keys
  Arrows/WASD move, Space waits, Z waits longer, E searches, C uses an
  app, a remedy or a trick, L spends Luck, U uses something, I shows you,
  O shows her, J the journal, M the map, ? help.

The tiles are drawn by the code below (PERSON_SHAPES, daffodil_art, ART
and draw_terrain).  --selftest builds every map, room, level and tune of
a few weeks without opening a window.
"""

